@startuml
' Virgin Bartending Game — Development View V2 (design-stage)
' UML package diagram: planned Python source organization and dependencies.
title Virgin Bartending Game — Development View (Package Diagram)

top to bottom direction
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam packageStyle rectangle
skinparam linetype ortho
skinparam nodesep 30
skinparam ranksep 38
skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam package {
  BackgroundColor #FAFAF4
  BorderColor #555555
}

package "bartending_game.application\n(app.py, states/)" as Application
package "bartending_game.gameplay\n(session.py, orders.py)" as Gameplay
package "bartending_game.models\n(customer.py, recipe.py, order.py)" as Models
package "bartending_game.minigames\n(base.py, pour.py, shake.py, garnish.py)" as Minigames
package "bartending_game.scoring\n(manager.py, strategies.py)" as Scoring
package "bartending_game.storage\n(loader.py, high_scores.py)" as Storage
package "bartending_game.presentation\n(assets.py, audio.py)" as Presentation

' Dashed arrows represent Python import / source-code dependencies.
Application ..> Gameplay : controls session
Application ..> Storage : reads / saves high scores
Application ..> Presentation : UI / audio

Gameplay ..> Models : uses orders and recipes
Gameplay ..> Minigames : creates and runs stages
Gameplay ..> Scoring : awards points
Gameplay ..> Storage : loads customer / recipe data

' Minigames receive primitive target values from GameSession,
' so no direct Minigames -> Models dependency is necessary.
Storage ..> Models : constructs loaded records

' Layout hints only; no additional semantic dependencies.
Minigames -[hidden]right- Scoring
Scoring -[hidden]right- Storage

legend bottom
  Dashed arrows show source-code dependencies between packages.
  GameSession passes recipe target values to minigames.
  Keep models and scoring independent of Pygame when possible.
  Tests, assets, and CI tooling live outside the runtime package.
endlegend
@enduml
